<div class="col-sm-12" align="center" style="border-radius: 20px; padding:10px; align-items: center;">
    <div class="row" align="center" style="word-wrap: break-word; font-weight: bold; padding-top: 10px;">
        <h1 style="word-wrap: break-word; font-weight: bold;">Mobile Server Documentation</h1>
    </div>
</div>
<br />

@*Background*@
<div class="col-sm-12" style="border-radius:20px; padding:10px; align-items:center;">
    <h3>Background</h3>
    <hr />
    <p>
        This is documentation on how to use the Febris Mobile Server. Start by downloading the Febris Mobile Server directly from the Febris Portal.
    </p>
    <p style="font-weight:bold">
        Not every aspect of the Febris mobile server is currently online. This will change in future releases.
    </p>
    <p style="font-weight:bold">
        If you violate your hardware's EULA by installing the Febris Mobile Suite, Febris takes no responsibility.
    </p>
</div>

@*Requirements*@
<div class="col-sm-12" style="border-radius: 20px; padding:10px; align-items: center;">

    <h3>Requirements</h3>
    <hr />
    <p>
        Needed Items:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            Android based Tablet (Android 10 [API 29] is optimal and minimum required OS, a phone can work but it's size can make operation a challenge.)
        </li>
        <li>
            Android based headset
        </li>
        <li>
            OTG cable and second USB cable (optional but sometimes required for headsets that have removed the normal installer)
        </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        If your Android device does not support the installation of software outside of their tightly controlled marketplace,
        you may not be able to install the Febris software and we appologize for the inconvience but there is nothing we can do about that situtation.
    </p>
</div>

@*Registering Device*@
<div class="col-sm-12" style="border-radius: 20px; padding:10px; align-items: center;">

    <h3>Registering a device</h3>
    <hr />
    <p>
        Registration of the device in the Febris system manages the automation of module distribution and data collection.
        The software needs to be running on the new mobile server before attempting to register it in your hardware system.
    </p>
</div>
<div class="col-sm-12" style="border-radius: 20px; padding:10px; align-items: center;">
    <p>
        Go to the configuration page and find the hardware license.
        This series of letters a numbers will be used when creating hardware on the Febris portal.
    </p>
</div>
@*Image*@
<div class="container-fluid" align="center">
    <img src="~/images/DocumentationImages/MobileServer/Config/MobileServerConfig_HardwareLicense.jpeg" alt="Hardware License" width="50%" />
    <p class="italic">Mobile Server Registration</p>
</div>


@*Setup*@
<div class="col-sm-12" style="border-radius: 20px; padding:10px; align-items: center;">

    <h3>Setting up your device</h3>
    <hr />
    <p>
        Currently there is no need to provide log in credentials for mobile server operations. So please do not feel any need to provide them.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you are a developer setup is as simple as selecting the developer option and saving the selection.
        From here on the device should be connected to your company’s account.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you are not a developer than the proper URL needs to be setup.
        Your IT administrator will have set up a private URL for your Febris operations.
        The URL can vary from a prefix, path, or port.
        If you do not know what these are please ask your IT Administrator.
        If the category does not exist, leave the section blank.
    </p>
    <p>
        Pressing update settings saves your new configuration.
    </p>
</div>
@*Image*@
<div class="container-fluid" align="center">

    <img src="~/images/DocumentationImages/MobileServer/Config/MobileServerConfig_SaveSettings.jpeg" alt="Mobile Server Config" width="50%" />
    <p class="italic">Mobile Server Config</p>
</div>



@*Adding a headset*@
<div class="col-sm-12" style="border-radius: 20px; padding:10px; align-items: center;">

    <h3>Adding a headset</h3>
    <hr />
    <p>
        A headset has to be registered via bluetooth. Please only register one device at a time.
    </p>
    <p>
        Configuration > Pair New Device > Scan
    </p>
</div>
@*Image*@
<div class="container-fluid" align="center">
    <img src="~/images/DocumentationImages/MobileServer/Config/MobileServerConfig_PairNewDevice.jpeg" alt="Pairing Page" width="50%" />
    <p class="italic">Pairing Page</p>
</div>
@*Image*@
<div class="container-fluid" align="center">
    <img src="~/images/DocumentationImages/MobileServer/Pairing/MobileServer_Pairing_Scan.jpeg" alt="Pairing Scan" width="50%" />
    <p class="italic">Pairing Scan</p>
</div>
<div class="col-sm-12" style="border-radius: 20px; padding:10px; align-items: center;">
    <p>
        Once the device you would like to pair is found it will be added to your device list.
    </p>
    <p>
        Now that the device is on your list you can either send the apk via Bluetooth or use the built in USB installer
    </p>
    <p>
        If you are using Bluetooth select the upload button on the right of that device and tell the tablet to upload the file over Bluetooth.
        You will then need to select the .apk file sent to the headset to install the companion application.
    </p>
</div>
@*Image*@
<div class="container-fluid" align="center">
    <img src="~/images/DocumentationImages/MobileServer/Pairing/MobileServer_Pairing_BTUpload.jpeg" alt="Bluetooth upload" width="50%" />
    <p class="italic">Bluetooth upload</p>
</div>
<div class="col-sm-12" style="border-radius: 20px; padding:10px; align-items: center;">
    <p>
        If you are using USB OTG, first put the two devices in development mode.
        Plug the OTG cable into the device running the mobile server.
        Plug the other end of the OTG cable into the headset/companion device.
        If the connection is made a few trust question may pop up on the screen.
        Approve all of the options.
        Two buttons will appear at the top of the Device Pairing Page.
        One will say “push and install” and the other will say “install”. First press “push and install”.
        This will upload the different apk variations of the companion software.
        If the push somehow fails in the middle of uploading some of the .apk files you can still try the install command.
        More than likely the needed file is already on your headset. If it works, there is no need to try to push the files again.
    </p>
</div>
@*Image*@
<div class="container-fluid" align="center">
    <img src="~/images/DocumentationImages/MobileServer/Pairing/MobileServer_Pairing_USBPushAndInstall.jpeg" alt="USB push and install" width="50%" />
    <p class="italic">USB push and install</p>
</div>

@*Removing a headset*@
<div class="col-sm-12" style="border-radius: 20px; padding:10px; align-items: center;">

    <h3>Removing a headset</h3>
    <hr />
    <p>
        A headset can be removed by pressing the trashcan icon on the left side of the device on the list.
    </p>
</div>
@*Image*@
<div class="container-fluid" align="center">
    <img src="~/images/DocumentationImages/MobileServer/Pairing/MobileServer_Pairing_DeviceRemoval.jpeg" alt="Device Removal" width="50%" />
    <p class="italic">Device Removal</p>
</div>


@*Potential certification issues*@
<div class="col-sm-12" style="border-radius: 20px; padding:10px; align-items: center;">

    <h3>Api connection issues</h3>
    <hr />
    <p>
        The R3 Certification lead to issues with some systems. Previously working applications stopped working in September of 2022. If you are facing it and cannot get a new certificate. There are a few easy steps you can take for a temporary workaround.
    </p>
    <p>
        Settings > Security > Encryption & Credentials > Trusted Credentials > Digital Signature Trust Co. (toggle)
    </p>
</div>
